I see where the confusion lies... you're talking about sudo, everyone
else here is talking about su. Big difference, in that su allows anyone
with the root password to execute *any* command as root in mostly the
same environment as if one were logged in as root (or *exactly the same*
if you give the single dash option to su).

sudo, on the other hand, allows anyone listed in the sudoers file to
execute predetermined commands (also listed in sudoers) as root using
their own password. Usually in a restricted environment to prevent nasty
things from happening behind the user's back...

So in short, on most (all?) Linux distributions:
su -c "make install"  =  will always work
sudo "make install"   =  might not work

